  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 5.068,
      "text": "The Wow signal lasted 72 seconds, the telescope's exact view limit.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 5.067,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 5.068,
      "end": 12.938,
      "text": "On August 15, 1977, Ohio State University's Big Ear telescope picked up the powerful anomaly.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 5.067,
          "end": 12.933,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 12.938,
      "end": 18.708,
      "text": "Astronomer Jerry R. [Ehman](/\u02c8im\u02c8\u00e6n/) discovered the signal while reviewing computer printout data.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Wow signal.jpg",
        "credit": "Credit: Big Ear Radio Observatory and North American AstroPhysical Observatory (NAAPO).",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wow_signal.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 12.933,
          "end": 18.7,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 18.708,
      "end": 24.283,
      "text": "Ehman circled the intensity reading [6EQUJ5](/s\u02c8\u026aks\u02c8ikj\u02c8uju\u02a4\u02c8Af\u02c8Iv/) and wrote Wow beside it.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Wow signal (large).jpg",
        "credit": "Credit: Big Ear Radio Observatory and North American AstroPhysical Observatory (NAAPO).",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wow_signal_(large).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 18.7,
          "end": 20.767,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 20.767,
          "end": 24.267,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 24.283,
      "end": 31.153,
      "text": "The signal frequency hit around fourteen twenty megahertz, matching the neutral hydrogen emission line.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Annotated HI line in terms of radial velocity.png",
        "credit": "IndyRishi",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Annotated_HI_line_in_terms_of_radial_velocity.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 24.267,
          "end": 31.167,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 31.153,
      "end": 36.898,
      "text": "In 2024, scientists found a magnetar flare hitting a gas cloud could cause it.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Artist\u2019s impression of the magnetar in the star cluster Westerlund 1.jpg",
        "credit": "ESO/L. Cal\u00e7ada",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artist%E2%80%99s_impression_of_the_magnetar_in_the_star_cluster_Westerlund_1.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 31.167,
          "end": 33.767,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 33.767,
          "end": 36.9,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 36.898,
      "end": 43.793,
      "text": "Despite decades of searches, the mysterious anomaly from the constellation Sagittarius has never recurred.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Sagittarius (sgr).tiff",
        "credit": "IAU and Sky & Telescope",
        "license": "CC BY 4.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sagittarius_(sgr).tiff"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 36.9,
          "end": 40.467,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 40.467,
          "end": 43.8,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 43.793,
      "end": 51.473,
      "text": "This natural fluke is what created the 72-second signal lasting the exact duration the telescope could watch.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 43.8,
          "end": 51.833,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
